  "pattern": "recursion-termination",
  "difficulty_rating": 1700,
  "explanation": "The recursive call `flatten_shallow(item, depth)` passes `depth` straight through instead of `depth - 1`, so the 'remaining depth' never actually decreases as the recursion goes deeper. The `depth == 0` base case that's supposed to stop the unwrapping is therefore unreachable from any positive starting depth — the function keeps recursing into every nested list until there's nothing left to flatten, fully flattening the whole structure to [1, 2, 3, 4, 5] instead of stopping after one level at [1, 2, [3, 4], 5]. It terminates (there's always a finite number of nesting levels), so nothing crashes or hangs — it just silently ignores the depth limit entirely.",
  "prompt": "flatten_shallow is meant to flatten only `depth` levels deep, leaving anything nested further alone. Step through and predict what flatten_shallow([1, [2, [3, 4]], 5], 1) returns.",
  "language": "python",
  "snippet": "def flatten_shallow(nested, depth):\n    if depth == 0:\n        return nested\n    result = []\n    for item in nested:\n        if isinstance(item, list):\n            result.extend(flatten_shallow(item, depth))\n        else:\n            result.append(item)\n    return result\n\nprint(flatten_shallow([1, [2, [3, 4]], 5], 1))",
